# Half-Life .357 revolver (Colt Python) - THE RIG THAT IS ACTUALLY INSTALLED
# at E:\XashWork\XashVR\valve\models\v_357.mdl. 44 bones, 8 seqs, 0xba7f43f7.
#
# WHY THIS CARD WAS THIN UNTIL 2 OCT 2026, AND WHY THAT MATTERED.
#
# A note written on 1 Oct recorded that the twelve cards in this directory
# "bind to the Half-Life VR Mod's rigs, 0 of 16 retail Half-Life models", and
# the work that followed - the hammer control, the crossbow optic, Part J's
# single action - was authored into cards/hd instead, against retail valve_hd.
#
# That was true and it was the wrong conclusion. THIS DIRECTORY IS THE OWNER'S
# ACTUAL GUNS. The install has these rigs in valve/models and the engine loads
# them; the retail HD rigs are the ones nobody plays. So three days of weapon
# work landed on twelve files the game never reads, and a headset session came
# back saying - correctly - that nothing had changed.
#
# MEASURED ON THIS FILE:
#   body bone        `python`, bone 11, 49.0% of the mesh    (same name as HD)
#   cylinder travel  1.10 rad on `revolver`, bone 14, in `reload`
#   trigger travel   0.75 rad on `trigger`, bone 12, in `fire1`
#   whole extent     20.02 units
#   muzzle           attachment on bone 22, `muzzle_pos`
#
# TRANSFERRED FROM cards/hd/v_357.card, AND SAY SO:
#   the hammer's 0.74 rad stroke, and the two control positions.
#
# The transfer is justified, not convenient. These are the same weapon: the
# same gun textures (v_python_Reference.BMP, bluesteelchrome.bmp), the same
# bone names, `python`/`hammer`/`revolver` at bones 11/13/14 in BOTH rigs, and
# the cylinder measuring 1.10 here against HD's 1.0996 - the same animation.
# Body-frame positions between the two rigs agree to within 0.4 units, which
# the grab radii below absorb.
#
# WHY THE HAMMER HAS TO BE STATED RATHER THAN MEASURED. On this rig vrcardgen
# reports `hammer` as "EXISTS BUT NEVER ANIMATED - give it a travel": the bone
# owns 2.6% of the mesh and not one of the 8 sequences moves it. The HD rig
# does animate it, 0.7365 rad in `fidget1`, and that is where 0.74 comes from.
# This is the one number in the card that is not off this file, and it is
# flagged here because the next person reading it will assume otherwise.
#
# STILL NOT VERIFIED IN A HEADSET. The two `control` positions are a thumb
# test, and a thumb that lands 0.4 units off is a control that never triggers.
# vr_diag prints the distance; read it rather than re-guessing.

weapon v_357
match  bones 44  seqs 8  hash 0xba7f43f7

# Bone 11, 673 verts, 49.0% of the mesh - the frame every position below is
# measured in.
body   "python"

# Six chambers, six SHELL bones. rpm 300 is NOT a measurement - a rate cannot
# be read off a model - and is carried from the HD card so both rigs of the
# same revolver behave alike. The double-action pull and the cylinder's own
# advance are the real limiter.
type   revolver  capacity 6  rpm 300

# THE HAMMER, RESTATED AS A PURE TURN. Joint 1's 0 is rest and its 1 is full
# cock, which is what the detent below assumes. No vertex moves and no bone is
# added; the motion is simply given a direction and a length, because this rig
# animates none.
synth hammer  from "hammer"  rewrite
      hinge  axis -1.0 0.0 0.0   travel 0.74

# JOINT 0 IS THE CYLINDER, because the `revolver` preset says so: it makes
# joint 0 the cylinder, sets feed.cyl_joint to 0, and makes joint 1 the hammer.
# Numbering them the other way round hands the hammer the cylinder's detent.
joint 0  bone "revolver"  hinge  travel 1.10  rest 0
    # The cylinder latch, as the magazine latch's catch - the only catch in
    # vr_feed's vocabulary meaning "this holds a part shut, it starts engaged,
    # and a thumb lets it go". Nothing outside VRGun_Preset and VRGun_Init
    # reads the id, so this is the latch idea under the only name the format
    # has for it. breakout 10000 lets a deliberate pull open it too.
    catch mag_latch  at 0.02  holds 1  breakout 10000

    # Open at 0.90, transferred: on the HD rig all six chamber mouths clear
    # the standing breech at 0.90 of the swing and not before. Same swing
    # here, so the same fraction. vr_joint's 3% hysteresis trips it at 0.93,
    # so the cylinder has to be swung right out - which is what a reload is.
    point cyl_open   at 0.90

    # Measured on THIS file: the cylinder's own vertex centroid.
    grab  point -0.44 -0.18 1.65  radius 2.5

# JOINT 1: the hammer, from the synth above.
joint 1  bone "hammer"  hinge  travel 0.74  rest 0
    # Full cock at 0.90 with 30% resist, which is what makes a hammer feel
    # like a hammer rather than a free-swinging flap. Crossing it sets
    # hammer_cocked and advances the cylinder one chamber - Part J's "chambers
    # advance one per cock" - and with break_cocked on the revolver preset it
    # is what gives the short 40% let-off instead of the long 85% pull.
    #
    # NO SEAR CATCH, deliberately. A revolver is double action, so
    # VRFeed_NeedsCocking returns 0 and feed.action_joint stays VRJ_NO_JOINT,
    # which is the only joint vr_feed ever releases a VRC_SEAR on. A sear here
    # would engage at full cock and nothing would ever let it go, leaving the
    # hammer stuck back for the rest of the weapon's life.
    point hammer_cock at 0.90  resist 0.30

    # Measured on THIS file: the hammer bone's own vertex centroid.
    grab  point -0.00 -0.28 1.39  radius 1.5

# THE THUMBPIECE AND THE SPUR. Both TRANSFERRED from the HD card, in the
# `python` frame - the rigs agree to within 0.4 units and these are the two
# numbers most worth re-measuring on this file if the thumb misses.
#
# Part G puts a cylinder latch on a face button (`kind button`) and the hammer
# on the stick (`kind lever`), because a hammer is DRAWN rather than released:
# every other control lets something go, and releasing a hammer the moment a
# thumb touched it is the trigger's job.
control mag_latch  at 0.92 0.37 0.01  finger thumb  kind button
control hammer     at 0.02 2.44 0.91  finger thumb  kind lever

# The firing chamber - the top one, under the barrel and under the hammer -
# and where the brass leaves. Both transferred; the chambers run along the
# bore axis, so cases come out along +Y, straight back, which is what tipping
# the muzzle up turns into a fall.
chamber at 0.01 0.33 0.85
eject   at 1.97 0.29 -0.60  dir 0.00 1.00 0.00
